  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN (descriptive, ~200 words) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Under new Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei — elected by the Assembly of Experts on March 9, 2026, following Ali Khamenei's assassination on February 28 — state media framing has not addressed the MOU's August 17 expiration directly. IRNA's August 22-23 coverage foregrounded Pezeshkian's defense of the expired agreement against internal hardline criticism, and Ghalibaf's "messages from neighboring nations" diplomatic-contact confirmation, a standard formulation used before both engagements and confrontations and not specific to the current juncture. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Tasnim News Agency (August 23) published IRGC General Staff language warning that any conflict resumption would face weapons "completely different from the past in every respect" and with "destructive power of warheads... far greater than those employed in previous wars." This formulation departs from the standard deterrence pattern by quantifying expected damage rather than merely asserting it. No parallel IRNA-verified corroboration of the specific capability claim appeared in the 48-hour window. → supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>

      <p>Tehran Times (August 23) ran a front-page characterization of Iran "systematically fortifying its defensive and offensive capabilities" — language consistent with the state-media posture documented in the weeks preceding the February 28, 2026 conflict opening. Pezeshkian's continued public defense of the now-expired MOU (IRNA cycle, August 22-23) against hardline criticism confirms the June 17-August 17 framework represented a genuine factional compromise, not a unanimous leadership position — its expiration leaves that internal division unresolved. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

